Found by mcp-hub-worker during k74 (2026-11-06). Since k74 a reload or shutdown that stops an upstream mid-warm rejects its start at once with 'upstream X: stopped'. Hub::_warm_upstream's catch still logs that at error level and calls _fetch_failed, marking the discarded (replaced/removed) object failed. Harmless, but noise. Done: a start rejected because of a stop is neither logged as an error nor recorded as a failed fetch; test with a reload during warming.

2026-11-06 11:35: the worker for k104/k89/k75 was stopped by an API safeguard error (request req_011CgmWnQmdqQskD15HPfsSg), not by a problem in the work. No change in the repo; its partial scratch work is in scratchpad/w-hub7. Back to backlog for the maintainer.

2026-11-06 (from k148): since k148 the start that a stop calls off rejects as 'upstream NAME: stopped' also when the stdio child ends on its EOF first (before: 'exited (code 0)'); _warm_upstream still logs it at error and _fetch_failed marks the upstream failed with 'stopped' -- the same for stdio and HTTP. Still open as described.
